course: dictionary
lesson: 14761
title: Forbidden by the rules
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: To proscribe something is to forbid it officially, by a law or a rule. It is a formal word, so you will often meet it in rules, laws and history books. When you see it, think: someone in charge has said no, and breaking the rule can bring punishment. «The school proscribed running in the corridors.» The clues are school and rules, so proscribed means officially forbidden.
explain: Proscribe can also be used for a group or a person. Then it means naming them as banned, so that joining them or helping them is a crime. «The government proscribed the group, so helping them became illegal.» Here the group is not just disliked: it is officially outlawed, and the law says helping it is a crime.
explain: Check the meaning by swapping it into the sentence. «The rules forbid littering» means the same as «The rules proscribe littering.» If your swap still makes sense, you have the right meaning. Proscribed is the past form, and proscribing is the form used with «is» or «are» happening now.
fact: Proscribe means to forbid something officially, by a law or a rule.
fact: «Proscribed» is the past form, as in «proscribed by law».
fact: When a group is proscribed, it is named as banned, and helping it is against the law.
fact: «Proscribed substances» means drugs or chemicals that the law bans.
fact: Proscribe is a formal word; in everyday talk people often say «ban» or «forbid» instead.
exercise: «The school proscribed running in the corridors.» What does proscribed mean here? | Given as a prize | Cleaned every single day | Painted in bright colours | *Officially forbidden by a rule
exercise: Which word is closest in meaning to proscribe? | Measure | Invite | *Forbid | Repair
exercise: «The government proscribed the group, so helping them became a crime.» What happens when a group is proscribed? | *They are named as banned, and helping them is a crime | They are sent a birthday card | They move to a new town | They win a medal from the council
test: «Swimming in the lake is proscribed by the park rules.» What does this tell you? | *Swimming there is officially forbidden | Swimming is free for everyone | Swimming is only for experienced sports people | The lake is brand new
test: Which phrase means banned by law? | Painted by hand | Chosen by a vote | Shared with friends | *Proscribed by law
exam: «The rules proscribed littering in the playground.» What does proscribed mean here? | Recycled by the class | Ignored by everyone | *Forbidden by a rule | Allowed with a smile
